.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1214 PM PST Sat Nov 21 2026
(This evening through Sunday night)

High clouds blanket the Bay Area and Central Coast as a weaker cold
front associated with a low pressure system located off the coast of
British Columbia approaches the California coast. KMUX radar has
shown isolated echoes over Sonoma County and the waters off of the
North Bay, while to our north, KBHX radar, which is situated along
the North Coast, shows a rainband running ahead of the frontal wind
change with showers behind the front. High resolution model output
suggests that this frontal rain band is expected to enter the North
Bay within the next couple of hours, starts edging into the SF Bay
Area this evening, and the Monterey Bay region early tonight. The
frontal rain band is expected to start to degrade as it moves
southward, but the high resolution models disagree on how fast that
process gets. RRFS model output suggests that the band becomes
scattered as it comes through the Bay Area tonight, while HRRR
output keeps the band relatively coherent into the Central Coast, by
which point the RRFS has dissipated the showers almost completely.
The main wind impact will be a shift in the wind direction from a
more southerly flow ahead of the front, to a more northwest flow
behind it. Post-frontal showers are possible behind the front,
especially on Sunday afternoon and evening, with the focus on the
offshore waters and the adjacent coastal regions.

High temperatures for the weekend remain cold with highs in the
lower to middle 50s across the inland valleys, the upper 50s to near
60 along the Bays and the Pacific coast, and the middle 40s to lower
50s in the higher elevations. Lows on Sunday morning remain chilly
with upper 30s to lower 40s in the inland valleys, the middle 30s in
the chillier spots of southern Monterey County, and the middle 40s
to lower 50s in the Bayshore and coastal regions.

Major coastal flooding concerns begin on Sunday as the combination
of high astronomical tides (the first in a sequence of "King Tides"
around November through January) and surge residual from thermal
expansion and storm surge effects leads to flooding concerns across
low-lying areas of the Bayshore areas and the Pacific Coast. A
Coastal Flood Advisory remains in effect for these areas through
Monday`s high tide cycle, when the San Francisco and San Pablo Bays
are upgraded to a Coastal Flood Warning while the rest of the
Pacific coast remains in Advisory level impacts. See the BEACHES
section for more details.

