.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1214 PM PST Sat Nov 21 2026
(Monday through next Friday)

Monday will represent a respite from the parade of storms, being
mainly dry if remaining a little cool with the inland valleys
hovering in the middle 50s to lower 60s. Use this day to prepare
your properties for the next storm, including clearing storm drains,
cleaning gutters, and making sure emergency kits are stocked.

The next system arrives on Tuesday and Wednesday, and this system
has had a pretty interesting run in the model trends. Compared to
yesterday around this time, the latest runs of the global models
suggest that the parent trough situated over the northern Pacific
south of Alaska will be a little weaker than what the previous day`s
output suggested. This, in turn, would lead to less of a moisture
tap for the incoming system, resulting in slightly lower
precipitation totals, and less of a pressure gradient over the
state, resulting in slightly weaker winds. That said, the impacts
will not disappear completely. Gusty winds and rainy conditions are
still expected across the region, with the rainfall impacts greatest
across the North Bay, where the current forecast puts rain totals
around the 1.5 to 3 inch range. Elsewhere in the SF Bay Area and
Monterey Bay region, forecast rainfall totals continue to fluctuate.
The best estimate would be around 0.5 to 1.5 inches, with higher
totals the closer you get to the coastal ranges. As for wind gusts,
the current forecast is for gusts to peak around 20 to 30 mph across
most of the region, with gusts up to 45 mph along the coast and
through favored gaps and passes. A couple notes about the impacts:
first, this system roughly coincides with the first cycle of the
King Tides, and rain runoff that reaches the Bays to find
historically high tide levels may contribute to coastal flooding
impacts. Second, this system will also impact the busy Thanksgiving
travel period and depending on how the system plays out, travel
delays on roads, railways, and airplanes are possible.

As the system clears out late Wednesday into early Thursday, the
Thanksgiving holiday itself should be generally dry, with an
incoming ridge helping to warm the inland valleys into the middle to
upper 60s. Another chance for rain arrives late Friday into the
upcoming weekend. Ensemble model cluster analysis begins to split
as varying solutions become present in the ensemble space, making
the forecast highly uncertain. Entering the first week of December,
the storm door looks to remain open with a lean towards
precipitation totals above the seasonal average.

