.AVIATION...
(18Z TAFS)
Issued at 919 AM PST Sat Nov 21 2026

Satellite imagery shows high clouds across the region with lingering
stratus possible in some patches across the interior, although
observation is difficult given the aforementioned high cloud cover.
Conditions should remain generally low-end VFR to higher-end MVFR as
the frontal system passes through the region, with the prevailing
southwesterly flow switching to a northwest breeze as the front
passes through. Current timing is for the front to go through the
North Bay now through 00Z, reach the main SF Bay terminals around 00-
04Z, and impact the Monterey Bay terminals around 05-09Z, at which
point the associated rain band will have broken into more scattered
to widespread showers. High resolution models are showing some
chances for stratus development tonight, although confidence is low
as the post-frontal environment should disrupt stratus formation
enough to limit potential development. Towards Sunday afternoon and
evening (beyond the end of the 24-hour TAF period), there is the
potential for post-frontal shower development, but exactly how
widespread those showers will be is unclear as the high resolution
models disagree.

Vicinity of SFO... VFR with mid- to high-level clouds through the
TAF period. A gentle southerly breeze develops this afternoon in
advance of a front that will bring light rain and a switch to
northwesterly flow later this afternoon into the evening hours.
Current thinking is for the front to arrive around 00-03Z with rain
continuing into the early overnight period. On Sunday, low
confidence in shower activity developing near the terminal,
especially in the afternoon hours.

SFO Bridge Approach... Similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals... VFR with mid- to high level clouds through
the evening hours, with a gentle southeast breeze slowly shifting
more to the southwest through the afternoon. The front is not
expected to arrive into the late evening and overnight hours (around
05-09Z) and by that time, shower coverage along the front is
expected to decrease. Still, showers are expected to develop at the
terminals and MVFR ceilings are possible. Showers may linger into
the post-frontal environment Sunday afternoon after the end of the
TAF period, but confidence is low.

